r example will use one  set of apps like use salesforce and a  few other apps  marketing will do the same thing they'll  use google ads they use marketo and  something  else the product team will use yet  another set of apps and so on and so  forth  across the organization you see this  happening  the problem here is that each department  has its own  island of data they have their own silo  of data but they're all disconnected  from each other  and they could all potentially have a  different  and even conflicting understanding of  critical business metrics  that should in theory be shared across  the organization  and everybody should have a common  understanding about them  so data professionals we we refer to  this as a  as an analytics governance problem but  it is a very  big problem it's a bigger problem than  that because it's also a value problem  because the fundamental value of  business intelligence a fundamental  belief that we all share  is that connecting data makes it way  more valuable  and and that it's only with connected  data that we get a better understanding  and a more comprehensive view  of how a business really functions  now this problem i'm afraid is only  going to get worse it's going to get  tougher to solve  according to idc within the next three  years 500 million  new business apps will be created so  if we think that the this is a tough  problem now if we think it's hard to  organize data now  it's only going to get harder it's going  to get harder a lot faster  so the question is what do we do  well something has to change according  to forrester  over 80 percent of structured data isn't  analyzed  and that's really unfortunate because it  means that over time  we're losing valuable insights and and  we're losing the opportunity to benefit  from connected data  but fortunately there is hope and that  hope is trend number three  data infrastructure is rapidly evolving  so data engineering technologies have  really come a long way they've changed  dramatically in the last few years  and and they've really raised the level  of analytics and insights there  that are achievable if i think back of  when i started my career you know more  than 20 years ago  the the technologies that were that were  considered the gold standard  then uh have are so so different  to to what we have today um the the  first set of of those technologies  is modern cloud databases databases like  bigquery  like snowflake even some of the aws  offerings these are  bigger faster cheaper databases where  you can store  massive amounts of data you can query  the data at a much lower cost  you can do it at a much higher level of  performance than these platforms of you  know years years past  but these databases are also getting  smarter you're seeing things like  augmented analytics  machine learning and other technologies  that automatically generate insights  and are and are integrated directly into  the database  the second thing you're seeing is that  once you have your data stored in a  modern cloud database  you can now take a more modern approach  to integrating and managing the data  and this modern approach doesn't depend  on physical data extracts  and it can transform data on the fly and  it's called elt  elt basically takes the traditional idea  of etl  and flips it on its head it makes the  management of the data ecosystem  a lot simpler a lot more flexible a lot  more agile  and you're going to find with this  approach that you'll need fewer data  extracts you'll be able to access data  in real time  you'll be able to access complete row  level data  your updates your data updates are going  to be simpler and a lot faster  but here's the key what enables this new  elt approach what makes it possible  is the growing importance of modeled  data  and what i mean by that is the the  ability to use  or or deploy a semantic model that  describes  all of your company's data a semantic  model that defines all of your business  logic  and with looker you use a technology  that we call look amel for your semantic  modeling  what's great about looking mel is that  it's a highly collaborative technology  and it allows tens or even hundreds of  data engineers to work together  on the same semantic model and what this  does is that it enables  real data teams as opposed to having  individuals  off on their own doing shadow i.t  so it's a very exciting time to work in  these in this industry  these data engineering technologies or  these advancements in data engineering  technologies  are really changing our world and our  lives as data professionals and  and uh they're they're allowing us to  bring much more data to the cloud  they're making it faster and easier to  deploy different types of data  and as i said they really allow your  workforce to to um  to work collaboratively and at scale  now let me show you a glimpse of how  quickly some of these cloud data  technologies are being adopted  what you're seeing here is a view across  looker's customer base  that shows the growth of different  database technologies so  you can see some of the classic database  technologies like postgres  and my sql and you know they're still  being used but if you pay attention to  the modern platforms like bigquery and  snowflake  the growth that we're seeing with those  technologies is just absolutely  remarkable  so if part of your motivation for  attending the session is that your  company is considering  making a transition or an investment in  a cloud database  now is the time to do that gartner says  that within the next three years 75  of all databases in existence will be  hosted in the cloud  and one of the main reasons for that  phenomenal growth of the cloud is the  ability to leverage  and manage data at the scale required  today  so with those three trends in mind let's  let's wrap up this section with a  description of what we think success  with data looks like  the first criteria is that data needs to  be complete and it needs to be  trustworthy  there needs to be confidence in the data  people need to be able to trust the  insights that your data platform is  delivering  we talk to companies all the time who  don't trust the data that their platform  is delivering because there's no  consistent business logic  across the thousands of workbook books  or spreadsheets or reports  that are being used by their employees  so what happens is that  instead of having productive  conversations about the data  you end up arguing over whose numbers  are correct  and in order to restore that trust  what's needed  is a centralized and reusable semantic  modeling layer  something like what lookers look at male  technology provides that allows everyone  to work with the same definition  of key business logic so there's never  ever any ambiguity  over what the data means there is only  one definition of customer there is only  one calculation for profit  there's only one definition of revenue  for example and everybody's speaking the  same language  the second criteria is that data should  be near real time  and that's really what modern cloud  databases allow us to do today  they make near real-time insights a  reality  and think of how valuable this is really  for any industry but to take retailers  for example imagine if retailers could  optimize  their digital advertising spend in real  time during the holiday shopping season  it's just  massively valuable the third criteria  is that data should not just have an  analytical purpose  it should also have an operational  purpose and this is a  really big departure from the way that  we've come to  think of data traditionally it means  that the  insights that your data platform is  generating should have the potential to  trigger  actions and the reason is that modern  business users don't just want to  understand the data they want to be able  to use data  to get something done to take some kind  of action  what that means is that we finally have  to actually  go beyond dashboards in the way that we  use data  data should feed other systems it should  feed  other machines to help people get real  work done to help drive the business  forward  it should not be used just to display it  on our report so let's take a look at a  few examples of how this actually works  in the real world  the first example is a company called  salesloft salesloft is a prospecting and  customer engagement platform based in  atlanta  and their customer success team monitors  customer health scores  and these scores look at product usage  and alert the customer success team of  any potential issues  early on now unfortunately this  information has to be manually  monitored and manually looked up  in order to catch low scores before  risking a customer churning  what they've done is now they've  replaced this manual system with an  automated system that they built using  looker so they combine  looker with the sales platform so  that when a customer health score drops  below a certain threshold  an alert automatically notifies the  customer success manager  of that problem and then automatically  triggers an  email within their engagement platform  to start re-engaging with that customer  this is massively valuable for uh for  sales loft it increases the relevance of  their customer communications it  increases the impact  of their customer communications and it  ultimately creates a better customer  experience  and and notice how this use case isn't  about showing  numbers on a graph or on a dashboard but  it's still an immensely valuable  data-driven application and just how  valuable well this application helped  sales staff  preserve 1.2 million dollars in arr  in the first four months alone  another example is car next door car  next door is a car sharing company in  australia  the car next door team was already using  looker they were getting value from  liquor  across a number of different areas  optimizing marketing delivering faster  support  more efficient product development but  then they used liquor in a way that was  pretty unexpected  um last year there were some pretty  severe hail storms that hit parts of  australia  and these storms caused really serious  damage to many of car next door's  vehicles  and of course as you can imagine this  resulted in higher repair  costs insurance losses from damaged  vehicles that they had to take out of  service  that also uncovered potentially risks  should something like this happen again  so  in order to reduce future downtime and  future losses  uh and to be able to continue delivering  an excellent customer experience  the car next door team quickly developed  a new application using looker  that would help them prepare for future  it weatherings so they do is that they  use liquor geospatial capabilities  and that allows them to identify cars  that are in locations that are  associated with weather events  and in combination with other  technologies like segment  and brays they can send alerts to  drivers to help them get to safety  so this is another example of using data  in a way that does not involve  displaying it on a report or on a  dashboard  but it does help card next door identify  at-risk cars  and at-risk drivers and it improves the  reliability of the service and improves  the customer experience too  and all of this is powered by look  finally an example that anybody in sales  will relate to  this is about slack slack has a product  that they built called midas  touch the problem that the midas touch  tool solves  is that their sales reps were spending  many many hours  every single week creating customer  slide decks so they were  building these decks by hand by  literally copying and pasting content  from their bi tool into google slides  this is very time consuming work it's  very repetitive  it's very prone to errors so of course  it's a perfect candidate for automation  so um they envisioned the midas tool the  midas touch tool excuse me at its core  as integration between slack and  salesforce and looker  and google slides and what the midas  touch tool does  is it allows the sales reps in slack to  search for customers  communicating with salesforce and then  request  a set of pre-populated personalized  slides  for that customer with data that's  pulled from looker  all without leaving slack so this means  that a sales rep can generate a  personalized report  for every single customer and every  single customer meeting that they go  into  with a single command as you can imagine  it makes them look like heroes  in every meeting that they go to and  it's a really interesting example  because it's such a great story of how  data can be used from multiple different  departments and can be brought together  in order to massively amplify the  experience for every customer  now these customer examples show how the  three trends that we reviewed earlier  are  all converging to create many great  opportunities for companies  to really build different kinds of data  experiences and for data leaders to  influence the the  their companies to drive their companies  in this direction  now when i see data leaders what does  that mean what is a data leader  well data leaders have lots of different  job titles the obvious ones like chief  data officer  head of analytics the vp of bi a data  analyst data engineer  but they can also be people that don't  necessarily have a data or analytics  title like a line of business leader  essentially anybody who can help shape  the data strategy for their organization  no matter the title these are the people  that are tasked with  creating a data culture and that means  that incorporating data  into not just our work lives but also  our personal lives  so you can see the examples of these  modern businesses that i shared  these data leaders are the ones who are  driving the company forward  uh and and that leads us to this idea  that data leadership  today really is business leadership  so the key is to find new use cases for  data  use cases that fall outside of the  conventional understanding of bi  this is a very interesting quote from  forester that talks about how leading  companies  are are thinking about data and how they  are driving growth  through differentiating experiences  products and services experiences  products and services it's really  interesting and what i find fascinating  about this quote  is that it talks about the value the  data can provide  but nowhere does it use terms like  analytics  or reporting or dashboards and the idea  is that data is no longer something that  is used exclusively for the purpose of  analyzing it on charts and graphs  data is now something that can be  infused into everyday business processes  that can  fuel operational workflows it can be  used to build new products to market it  can be used to make people smarter  to optimize your operations to create  new revenue streams  and and this is very exciting because  what we're talking about is expanding  the idea of traditional bi and really  bringing it into the modern era the era  of data-driven experiences  so what can you do as a data leader to  initiate this change within your  organization and to bring your company  into this  era of data driven experiences  well first of all think of modern data  technologies  think of your current technology stack  what are the technologies that comprise  it  think of your business users do they  trust the insights that are delivered by  your data platforms  are these insights real time  understanding the data technologies  landscape is obviously fundamental for  data leaders  but not just to maintain technical  parity but also to be able to innovate  and deliver cutting-edge solutions using  these modern data technologies  um if you're a younger organization  there's a good chance that you're  already digital that you're already  cloud native  but if you're a more established  business and maybe  it's been it's been a longer road to  migrate to the cloud and migrate to  modern technologies  you need to start this digital  transformation somewhere where do you  start  choose a department uh choose a team  choose a particular project  to start getting technologies and people  in place the key really is to find that  right use case  and go after it secondly  imagine new experiences with data as i  said before  data is no longer meant as something  just to be analyzed  uh it's it's really uh something that  is meant to lower the barriers for your  users to fuel operational workflows to  deliver superior experiences for your  employees  for your customers the founder of looker  lloyd tab  has a very powerful guiding principle  that says that  great software is an act of empathy and  what this means is it means being  aware of how users use technology today  of what tools you're using today and  meeting them  with data in those tools it means  meeting our workforce and customers  where they already  work and bringing trustworthy data into  the tools that they already use  and they already trust and finally  consider a new role for you  uh data leaders have an amazing  opportunity today to really  influence the direction of their  organizations in very significant ways  every part of an organization that's  going through a digital transformation  is really fueled by data in some way and  data professionals who are  capable of not just solving technology  problems but also solving business  problems  by using data to create new products to  discover new value streams are have  become immensely immensely valuable  and we see this within our own customer  base too all the time  we see data leaders who have  really delivered value to their  organization and have moved on to  business leadership  and really the idea is that data leaders  are positioned to be the business  leaders of our time  so what that means and to say in a very  light-hearted way  is that the data nerds have won data has  been infused into so many moments of our  daily lives  at work at home and these data  experiences are brought to us by data  leaders  yes engineers and analysts but also as i  said data savvy  marketing people sales people product  people  all of them are data nerds and all of  them are data leaders and as i mentioned  we see this  within our own customers we have a lot  of examples of data savvy individuals  who were able to rapidly advance their  careers  because of the value that they created  for their organizations  for their customers through the use of  data um  we can we we have customers folks who  were data analysts  just a few years ago but they became  highly recruitable  and are now in key business leadership  positions at their organizations or  other organizations  so to wrap up i hope you recognize the  enormous opportunity in front of you  today  both for your company but also for your  career data is more valuable than ever  and people who can find a way to lead  with data are in high demand  um we're seeing more demand than ever  from that data hungry workforce and from  a customer base  we see more and more data that's being  generated by an explosion of sas  applications and we continue to see data  infrastructure evolving  and creating new possibilities for using  data in ways that were just  unimaginable not that long ago  so with that i want to thank you for  your time and attention and i invite you  to learn more about looker and engage  with our team so that we can discuss  your data and analytics objectives thank  you
 

